<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="algebra-vocabulary">
<title>The Language of Algebra</title>
<section xml:id="algebra-words">
<title>Variables, coefficients, constants and terms</title>
<p>A <term>variable</term> is a letter or symbol representing a number which may vary or is unknown. In <m>3x+5</m>, <m>x</m> is the variable.</p>
<p>A <term>coefficient</term> is the numerical factor multiplying a variable or its power. In <m>7x^2</m>, the coefficient is <m>7</m>; in <m>-x</m>, it is <m>-1</m>.</p>
<p>A <term>constant</term> is a fixed value. In <m>3x+5</m>, the constant term is <m>5</m>.</p>
<p>A <term>term</term> is one of the parts of an expression separated by addition or subtraction at the outermost level. Keep the sign with the term: <m>3x-5</m> has the signed terms <m>3x</m> and <m>-5</m>.</p>
<example xml:id="algebra-vocabulary-example"><title>Read an expression</title>
<p>In <m>4x^2-3x+7</m>, the terms are <m>4x^2</m>, <m>-3x</m>, and <m>7</m>. The coefficients are <m>4</m> and <m>-3</m>, and the constant term is <m>7</m>.</p>
</example>
</section>
<section xml:id="algebra-operation-words">
<title>Sum, difference, product, fraction and quotient</title>
<p>A <term>sum</term> is the result of adding two or more terms or quantities. <m>3x+5</m> is the sum of two terms.</p>
<p>A <term>difference</term> is the result of subtracting one quantity from another. In <m>7x-4</m>, <m>7x</m> is the minuend and <m>4</m> is the subtrahend.</p>
<p>A <term>product</term> is the result of multiplying two or more <term>factors</term>. <m>3x</m> is the product of the factors <m>3</m> and <m>x</m>.</p>
<p>A <term>quotient</term> is the result of dividing a <term>dividend</term> by a non-zero <term>divisor</term>. In <m>12\div 3=4</m>, the dividend is <m>12</m>, divisor is <m>3</m>, and quotient is <m>4</m>. The dividend and divisor are not called "two factors".</p>
<p>A <term>fraction</term> is an expression with a numerator above a non-zero denominator. In <m>\frac{x+2}{5}</m>, the numerator is <m>x+2</m> and the denominator is <m>5</m>. A fraction can represent division.</p>
<example xml:id="algebra-terms-factors-example"><title>Terms are not factors</title>
<p><m>3x+5</m> is a sum of two terms. <m>3x</m> is one term but a product of two factors. <m>3(x+2)</m> is a product of two factors; expanding gives the sum <m>3x+6</m>.</p>
</example>
<p>Say it aloud: <em>Term plus term gives a sum. Factor times factor gives a product. Dividend divided by divisor gives a quotient.</em></p>
</section>
<section xml:id="algebra-language-practice">
<title>Practise the language</title>
<exercise xml:id="algebra-language-exercise-one"><statement><p>Name the three terms, the coefficients and the constant term in <m>6a^2-5a+9</m>.</p></statement><answer><p>Terms: <m>6a^2</m>, <m>-5a</m>, <m>9</m>. Coefficients: <m>6</m> and <m>-5</m>. Constant term: <m>9</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
<exercise xml:id="algebra-language-exercise-two"><statement><p>Describe <m>4(2x-3)</m> as a product, then expand it and identify the terms.</p></statement><answer><p>Factors: <m>4</m> and <m>(2x-3)</m>. Expanded expression: <m>8x-12</m>; terms: <m>8x</m> and <m>-12</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
<exercise xml:id="algebra-language-exercise-three"><statement><p>In <m>\frac{3x+6}{3}</m>, identify numerator, denominator, dividend and divisor.</p></statement><answer><p>Numerator and dividend: <m>3x+6</m>. Denominator and divisor: <m>3</m>.</p></answer></exercise>
</section>
</chapter>